            <div class="intro-card">
              <p class="intro-eyebrow">Family-owned on the Suncoast since 2004</p>
              <h2 id="walk-in">Bradenton &amp; Sarasota Curbless &amp; Walk-In Showers</h2>
              <p class="intro-lead">A shower you step into rather than climb over — designed, permitted and built by one in-house crew.</p>
              <p>A walk-in shower is the change people notice every single morning. No tub wall to swing a leg over, no sliding door track collecting grime, and a room that looks twice its size the moment the glass replaces a curtain. Curbless takes it one step further: the bathroom floor runs straight into the shower with nothing to step over at all.</p>
              <p>What makes that work is not visible in any photo. A shower with no curb has no backstop, so every drop of water is controlled by the fall built into the pan, the drain that carries it, and the membrane that keeps it out of your framing. Get those three right and a curbless shower is drier than the curbed one it replaced. Get them wrong and you have a wet bathroom floor for as long as you own the house.</p>
              <p>We build both here, and we will tell you plainly which one your room wants. Twenty-two years and more than 1,300 projects in, we have opened up enough Florida bathrooms to know what the slab is likely to allow before we start cutting it.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="stat-tiles">
              <div class="stat-tile"><b>0"</b><span>Threshold on a curbless build</span></div>
              <div class="stat-tile"><b>24 hrs</b><span>Flood test before tile</span></div>
              <div class="stat-tile"><b>Schluter®</b><span>Certified installers</span></div>
              <div class="stat-tile"><b>Full</b><span>Workmanship warranty</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="types">The showers we build</h2>
            <p>Every one of these is a walk-in. Which one fits comes down to your slab, your drain, and how long you plan to be in the house:</p>

            <div class="pj-grid">
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Curbless &amp; zero-threshold</h3>
                <p>No curb, no lip, no trip hazard — the bathroom floor carries straight into the shower over a recessed or engineered sloped pan, usually draining to a linear drain at the back wall.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Low-curb walk-in</h3>
                <p>The workhorse: a 3–5" tiled curb, frameless glass, and none of the slab work. Most of the look and most of the daily comfort of curbless, at a noticeably lower number.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Tub-to-shower conversion</h3>
                <p>The alcove tub nobody has used in fifteen years, rebuilt as a roomy walk-in shower in the same footprint — the most requested upgrade in Bradenton and Sarasota. <a href="/blog/tub-to-shower-conversion-bradenton/">What a conversion involves</a>.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Wet rooms &amp; doorless</h3>
                <p>Shower and tub sharing one waterproofed, fully tiled zone, or a walk-in long enough to skip the door entirely. The whole area gets the shower's waterproofing, not just the pan.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Accessible &amp; roll-in</h3>
                <p>A 36" clear opening, a 1/2" beveled threshold or none at all, a bench, and real blocking set in the walls for grab bars now or later — built to read as a good bathroom, not a clinical one.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pj-c">
                <h3>Frameless glass enclosures</h3>
                <p>Templated off finished tile rather than off framing, so the panels sit plumb and the seams close. Coated glass as standard — untreated glass spots within a season in water this hard.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="curbless">Curbless on a Florida slab</h2>
            <p>Almost every home between Palmetto and Venice is slab-on-grade, and that is the whole story of curbless construction here. A shower pan needs roughly a quarter inch of fall per foot to drain. In a house with a crawlspace or joists you borrow that depth from the framing. On a slab there is nothing to borrow from — so the depth has to be made.</p>
            <p>There are two honest ways to do it, and we price whichever your room actually allows:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Recess the slab.</strong> Saw-cut the shower footprint, break out the concrete, re-route the drain, and re-pour low so the finished tile lands flush with the bathroom floor. The cleanest result, and the one that needs a permit and an inspection.</li>
              <li><strong>Build the floor up.</strong> An engineered sloped assembly over the existing slab, with the rest of the bathroom floor raised to meet it and the transition handled at the doorway. No concrete work — but it only works where the door swing and the threshold height allow it.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>A <strong>linear drain</strong> set against the back wall does the rest. Because the entire pan can then slope in one direction instead of four, large-format tile can run into the shower uncut, the fall is gentler underfoot, and there is no dish-shaped low point at the centre of the floor.</p>

            <blockquote>
              <p><strong>The detail that decides it:</strong> where your existing drain sits. A curbless shower wants its drain at the wall, and moving one on a slab means cutting concrete — which is exactly the work a curb exists to avoid. We check the drain location at the walkthrough, before anyone falls in love with a drawing, because it is the difference between a straightforward build and a four-figure line item.</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2 id="why-vl-pro">Why homeowners choose VL Pro</h2>
            <p>There are a lot of remodelers between Palmetto and Venice. Here is what actually differs about working with us:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>One in-house crew.</strong> The same people demo the alcove and set the last piece of glass trim. No subcontractor handoffs, and nobody blaming the last trade.</li>
              <li><strong>Schluter®-certified waterproofing.</strong> Every pan is built on a certified bonded membrane and flood-tested for 24 hours before a single tile goes on. See <a href="/services/waterproofing-subfloor-preparation/">how the assembly goes in</a>.</li>
              <li><strong>Both versions priced.</strong> You get the curbed number and the curbless number for your room at the walkthrough, so the decision is made on a price rather than a feeling.</li>
              <li><strong>Permits pulled and inspected.</strong> We file as the licensed contractor of record with Manatee County, Sarasota County or your city building department, and we meet the inspector.</li>
              <li><strong>Glass measured off finished tile.</strong> Templated after the tile is set, never off the framing — which is why our panels close instead of needing a bead of caulk to hide the gap.</li>
              <li><strong>Certified for the surfaces we install.</strong> Large-format tile, natural stone, XL porcelain slab and glass tile, and members of the National Tile Contractors Association.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="process">How your shower gets built</h2>
            <p>Every shower we build runs through the same four phases, so you always know where the project stands and what happens next.</p>

            <div class="steps">
              <div class="step">
                <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
                <h3>Walkthrough &amp; detailed estimate</h3>
                <p>We measure the room, find the existing drain, check what the slab and the door threshold will allow, and talk through curbed versus curbless honestly. You leave with an itemized scope, both prices and a start date — not a range. No cost, no obligation.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="step">
                <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
                <h3>Design, selections &amp; permit</h3>
                <p>Drain type and position, pan slope, bench and niche layout, tile, fixtures and glass are settled and ordered before anything comes apart. If the build cuts the slab or moves a drain, we pull the permit while the materials are in transit.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="step">
                <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
                <h3>Demo, slope &amp; waterproofing</h3>
                <p>Out comes the tub or the old pan. We recess or build up the floor, set the drain, and install blocking for the bench and grab bars while the walls are open. Then the membrane goes on, gets tied in at every corner and at the entry, and the pan is filled and held for 24 hours before tile.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="step">
                <span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
                <h3>Tile, glass &amp; walkthrough</h3>
                <p>Tile is set to a planned grid so the cuts land where nobody looks, grout is sealed, fixtures go on, and the glass is templated off the finished tile and installed last. We clean the room, walk it with you, and fix everything on your list before we call it done.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="cost">What a walk-in shower costs in Bradenton and Sarasota</h2>
            <p>Three things move the number: whether there is a curb, whether the drain moves, and what goes on the walls. Most walk-in showers in this market run between <strong>$9,000 and $20,000</strong> and take two to four weeks, plus two to three weeks for glass. These are typical ranges for our area — your estimate is built to your actual room.</p>

            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Scope</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Timeline</th><th>Includes</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Low-curb walk-in</td>
                  <td>$9K – $14K</td>
                  <td>2–3 weeks</td>
                  <td>Existing footprint and drain, waterproofed pan, tiled curb and walls, niche, frameless glass panel.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><span class="row-us">Curbless walk-in</span></td>
                  <td>$13K – $20K</td>
                  <td>3–4 weeks</td>
                  <td>The above plus slab recess or built-up sloped assembly, linear drain, flush entry, bench and grab-bar blocking.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Wet room / primary suite</td>
                  <td>$20K and up</td>
                  <td>4–6 weeks</td>
                  <td>Larger waterproofed zone, two shower positions or a tub inside the glass, stone or slab walls, custom enclosure.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>Two items sit outside those ranges often enough to name: relocating a drain on slab adds roughly <strong>$2,000–$6,000</strong>, and if demolition uncovers a rotted subfloor or a pan that has been leaking for years, you get a photo of it and a price for the repair before we carry on.</p>

            <h2 id="accessible">Aging in place, done well</h2>
            <p>Most of the curbless showers we build are not for a wheelchair today. They are for a homeowner who intends to stay in the house, and who would rather do the work once — while the walls are already open — than do it under pressure after a fall.</p>
            <p>That means a wide opening, a pan with no lip at all, slip-resistant tile in the pan rather than polished stone, a bench sized to actually sit on, and real wood blocking set behind the tile so grab bars can be mounted anywhere on those walls later without opening anything up. Where the build is fully accessible, ADA guidance asks for a 60" × 30" clear interior, a 36" opening, and no threshold over 1/2" with that half inch beveled.</p>
            <p>None of it has to look medical. Specified properly, the bar is a towel bar, the bench is a ledge, and the flush floor just reads as a bigger room.</p>

            <h2 id="offer">Save on your project</h2>
            <h3>$1000 off your project, plus a free in-home design consultation</h3>
            <p>Ask about the current offer when you book. Terms and restrictions apply, and it is valid on projects over $10,000. The in-home consultation is free either way — we measure the room, find the drain, and price the curbed and curbless versions side by side before you commit to anything.</p>

